---
/**
 * Where the figures come from, how they are checked, and what is deliberately not here.
 *
 * This site's whole posture is that a reader can check it. That argument previously lived only in
 * the repository's README files, which is to say it was available to contributors and to nobody
 * else. It is a page now.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { loadCorpus } from "../lib/corpus.ts";
import { loadFeed } from "../lib/feed.ts";
import { count, millions, pct } from "../lib/format.ts";

const { bundle, verification, forecastable } = loadFeed();
const corpus = loadCorpus();
const s = bundle.statewide;
const meta = bundle.projection;
---

<Base
  title="Method"
  description={`How the figures on this site are produced: the department's own FY${bundle.fiscal_year} model, re-run and checked against ${verification.comparisons.length} reference scenarios before the site is allowed to build.`}
  section="method"
>
  <h1>Method</h1>
  <p class="sub">
    Where every figure comes from, what checks it passed to get here, and what this site
    deliberately does not show.
  </p>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>The formula is computed twice, on purpose</h2>
    <p class="note">
      Ohio's Fair School Funding Plan is implemented in this repository twice: once in Rust, in
      <code>crates/foundation</code> and <code>crates/project</code>, and once in TypeScript, in
      <code>src/lib/policy.ts</code>. Two implementations of one formula is normally a bad trade —
      they drift, and the one nobody runs is the one that is wrong.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      What makes it acceptable is that <strong>the Rust is authoritative and the TypeScript has to
      prove it agrees</strong>. The feed carries {verification.comparisons.length} scenarios and {
        verification.forecasts.length
      } forecasts computed by the Rust, and the TypeScript must reproduce every one of them —
      against the real {count(s.districts)}-district panel, to within a dollar across seven
      billion — before this site is allowed to build. It reproduced all of them for this build.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      That is not a formality. Wiring the check up found a bug <em>in the Rust</em>: a district
      pulled below a raised minimum state share was taking the branch meant for districts already
      on the floor, and having its published share scaled by the ratio of the minimums instead of
      by the floor itself. It was worth $4.7 million, and 273 Rust tests did not catch it, because
      the wrong answer is still an increase and still confined to districts below the new floor.
      The second implementation disagreed, and the disagreement was the signal.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      The check runs in two places for two different reasons. At <strong>build</strong> it is a
      gate: it fails the build, so a drifted formula is never published. On <strong
        ><a href="/scenario">the scenario routes</a></strong
      > it runs again in the browser, against the panel actually served — because that file can be
      replaced without a rebuild, and a check that only ran where the answer was already known
      would be decorative.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>What is a model, and what is a record</h2>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table class="prose">
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th>The funding figures</th>
            <td>
              A <strong>model</strong>, and the department's rather than this repository's. The
              per-district figures are read from its FY{bundle.fiscal_year} TRAD State Foundation
              Funding Calculator, not recomputed — with one exception, below. What gets recomputed
              here is what a <em>different</em> decision would produce. It is a projection of what
              districts will be paid, not a record of what they were.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>The five-year forecast figures</th>
            <td>
              A <strong>record</strong>. Audited actuals from each district's own filing — money
              that changed hands. These are the only figures on this site that are not computed,
              which is why they have <a href="/districts">their own route per district</a> and are
              never placed as a check on the model. The two are differently constructed and are not
              comparable line for line.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>The outcome figures</th>
            <td>
              A <strong>measurement</strong>, of pupils and not of districts. The 2024-25 Ohio
              School Report Card. Every association drawn from it is a correlation over {
                s.outcomes ? count(s.outcomes.districts) : "—"
              } districts and none of them identifies an effect. <a href="/outcomes">Why that
              matters here in particular</a>.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>The property tax figures</th>
            <td>
              A <strong>record</strong>, and from a different department. Table SD-1 is published
              by the Ohio Department of Taxation rather than the Department of Education, which
              makes it the one place two arms of the state describe the same district
              independently — and where they overlap they agree, to 0.01 mills across all 606
              districts carrying both. Two tax years, because H.B. 920 is a mechanism that only
              exists as a change.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>The spending-by-function figures</th>
            <td>
              A <strong>record</strong>, from the report card, per pupil, on unweighted ADM. Not
              the audited actuals beside them on the same page: different source, different basis,
              and not summable with them.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>The projection</th>
            <td>
              A <strong>forecast</strong>, and the only thing on this site that is. Enrollment is
              carried forward by a damped trend and the formula is re-run at it; nothing projects
              the formula, the appropriation, or assessed valuation. Its interval is drawn as the
              headline rather than as a disclaimer, for reasons below.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>The one thing here that is computed</h2>
    <p class="note">
      Base cost is the exception to the row above. It is not a rate the state sets: R.C. 3317.011
      assembles it for each district from statutory staffing ratios applied to that district's own
      grade-band enrollment, priced at statewide average salaries — twenty-two elements in five
      sub-components. <code>crates/foundation</code> runs that statute, and every district page
      shows the result rather than the total.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Which is a claim, so it is reconciled rather than asserted. The department publishes its own
      aggregate for every district; the build-up is shown beside it with the difference stated. The
      worst of the {count(s.districts)} is <strong>$1.09</strong> on figures in the millions, and
      the residuals cancel to nothing statewide — twenty-two elements each rounded at the point the
      department rounds them. The reproduction is checked across the whole panel in
      <code>crates/foundation</code>'s tests before this site is allowed to build.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      This is the lever the <a href="/wiki/scenario/fsfp-input-year-refresh">cost input refresh</a>
      question turns on: the ratios and the ADM definitions are fixed in statute, and the prices are
      a reference year the General Assembly chooses. H.B. 110 priced FY2022 from FY2018 salaries,
      H.B. 33 refreshed the inputs to FY2022, and H.B. 96 held them there through FY{
        bundle.fiscal_year
      }.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Why the forecast leads with a range</h2>
    <p class="note">
      A forecast rendered as "$7.13B (±3.4%)" is read as $7.13B. The interval becomes a disclaimer
      the eye skips, which is worse than showing no interval at all, because it lends a forecast
      the authority of a measurement. So the tile's large number is the <strong>range</strong> and
      the central estimate is the small line underneath, labelled as one path through the band. In
      the chart the band is a mark rather than shading, its centre line is dashed, only the two
      bounds are direct-labelled, and the y axis — truncated, because a band a few percent wide is
      invisible against a zero baseline — says so on its face.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      The band is <strong>not</strong> any district's own historical variability; three
      observations cannot give that. It is the {meta ? meta.prior_source : "cross-sectional spread"}
      {meta ? `, σ = ${pct(meta.sigma, 2)} a year` : ""}, widened by the square root of the horizon
      because growth errors compound. Both ends are computed by re-running the whole formula at
      that enrollment rather than by scaling the middle: the guarantee is a <code>max</code>, and a
      district can be on formula at one end of the band and on the guarantee at the other.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      The forecast card is always below the simulation and is never merged with it, and there is
      deliberately no figure anywhere on this site that adds them together. Re-running the formula
      with a lever moved is exact; projecting enrollment six years out is not, and a combined
      number would inherit the second's error while wearing the first's precision.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Provenance</h2>
    <p class="note">{bundle.provenance}</p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table class="prose">
        <tbody>
          <tr><th>Bundle contract</th><td>{bundle.contract_version}</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Districts in the model</th><td>{count(s.districts)}</td></tr>
          <tr>
            <th>Reference scenarios reproduced</th>
            <td>{verification.comparisons.length} of {verification.comparisons.length}</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>Reference forecasts reproduced</th>
            <td>
              {forecastable
                ? `${verification.forecasts.length} of ${verification.forecasts.length}`
                : "check did not pass — no band is drawn on this build"}
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr><th>Price index</th><td>{bundle.deflator?.label ?? "none"}</td></tr>
          <tr>
            <th>Corpus</th>
            <td>
              {corpus.nodes.length} nodes, {corpus.classes.length} classes,{" "}
              {corpus.sources.length} <a href="/wiki/source">sources</a>
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Every source the corpus draws on is listed at <a href="/wiki/source">/wiki/source</a>, and
      the feed itself is downloadable at <a href="/data">/data</a>. Thirteen of the retrieved files
      are pinned by SHA-256 in the repository, so a claim can be traced to a byte-identical copy of
      the publication it came from.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>What is not here</h2>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table class="prose">
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th>Year-over-year funding</th>
            <td>
              The department publishes one foundation calculator at a time and replaces rather than
              archives it, so no FY{bundle.fiscal_year - 1} per-district payment figure exists in
              this repository to compare against. What the district pages show instead is the FY{
                bundle.fiscal_year
              } formula held fixed and run at each of three enrollment years, which isolates the
              enrollment channel exactly — something two years of published totals could not do,
              because the formula moved between them too.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>The regime history</th>
            <td>
              Walking the numbers from the Foundation Program through to the Fair School Funding
              Plan needs a historical series the corpus does not yet have. The regimes themselves
              are documented — <a href="/wiki/funding-regime">five of them</a> — but without
              per-district figures attached.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>Property values under a scenario</th>
            <td>
              Assessed valuation is held fixed in every scenario. Local capacity is 60% valuation,
              so a proposal that moves property values is out of reach: the corpus has one
              observation per district and cannot project it.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>Tax received, as opposed to charged</th>
            <td>
              Table SD-1 reports what is <em>charged</em> for a tax year. Collection happens across
              the following calendar year in two settlements, so a levy passed in November is
              charged in full and arrives across two fiscal years. Every ratio here that puts a
              charge over a year's spending inherits that gap, and it is largest exactly where a
              levy has just passed — which is one of the three districts charged more than it
              spends.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>Community schools and scholarships</th>
            <td>
              The deduction that routes money from a resident district to a community school or a
              scholarship is not modelled here. The {count(s.districts)} districts in this feed are
              the traditional districts in the state's own calculator.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Checking it yourself</h2>
    <p class="note">
      Everything here is reproducible from the repository. The feed is regenerated with one
      command, the site is built with a second, and continuous integration diffs the committed feed
      against a fresh one so the two cannot silently disagree.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Total state foundation aid in this model is{" "}
      {millions(s.realized_aid_total).replace("+", "")} across {count(s.districts)} districts, of which {millions(s.guarantee_total).replace("+", "")}
      {" "}is paid by the guarantee to {s.on_guarantee} districts. If a figure on this site
      disagrees with the department's own, that is a defect and the repository is where to file it.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
